{"task":"transcribe","language":"english","duration":577.25,"text":"Just to take a customer workload for a personal assistant, you need to create 10,000 sandboxes a second. You need to run almost a million sandboxes. And they're not going to last very long. They'll just last for, like, two or three minutes. But when they come up, you want them to be interactive in less than a second. That's what this workload really demands. And the other interesting thing on scale is you actually cannot get all of this scale from a single cloud platform. The world is actually getting crunched on CPUs as well, not just GPUs. So one thing we spend a lot of time doing is actually aggregating cloud capacity from a bunch of hyperscalers and neoclouds. So our users have access to CPU capacity across the world when they need it. But we cannot rely on one cloud provider to do that. OK, so where is all this going? I hinted at this earlier, but the harnesses are really good at making use of dynamic environments. And what this is leading to is a lot of separation of the brains, which is the harness, from the hands, which is the sandbox or the runtime where stuff is running. And what this means, I have an example here, is the harnesses are going to be able to spin up environments based on what they need right now. They're good at snapshotting them and keeping them around as artifacts that they need later. And again, adjusting all of this as necessary. So I think this points at an interesting evolution of the pets to cattle analogy we're all familiar with. To recap, in the olden days, people thought of services like pets. Each one was bespoke. You had to manage it. You had to reason about this individual deployment specifically. And then over time, with containerization and Kubernetes, people started to think about it as homogeneous templates of these servers. They're all the same. You can scale them up and down. If one of them fails, it's not an issue because our system can heal from that. And I think this is where a lot of people deploying agents are today. They're kind of using these homogeneous environments and scaling up these agents. But I think that's really limiting because the models are so good at reasoning about dynamic environments, snapshotting, creating lots of environments when needed and working across them. So what does the new world look like? One analogy here is it kind of looks like an ocean of microbes, of all these organisms that are self-evolving and working with each other. And credit to Swix for the analogy here. But one thing I'm really excited about is as you give agents these really rock-solid primitives for compute and storage and networking, they will be able to actually do a lot more by doing the stuff I talked about, like spinning up lots of environments, working with each other, and being very dynamic. So yeah, I and us at Modal really think that great primitives let your agents do more. And yeah, if you're interested in talking about this more, reach out to us. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you.  Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you. Thank you.  Thank you.","segments":[{"id":0,"seek":0,"start":0.0,"end":6.71999979019165,"text":" Just to take a customer workload for a personal assistant, you need to create 10,000 sandboxes","tokens":[50364,1449,281,747,257,5474,20139,337,257,2973,10994,11,291,643,281,1884,1266,11,1360,42115,279,50700],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.26443955302238464,"compression_ratio":1.5859030485153198,"no_speech_prob":0.04808449000120163},{"id":1,"seek":0,"start":6.71999979019165,"end":7.71999979019165,"text":" a second.","tokens":[50700,257,1150,13,50750],"temperature":0.0,"avg_logprob":-0.26443955302238464,"compression_ratio":1.5859030485153198,"no_speech_prob":0.04808449000120163},{"id":2,"seek":0,"start":7.71999979019165,"end":11.760000228881836,"text":" You need to run almost a million sandboxes.","token